From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TBI Lab runtime <runtime@tbi-lab.local>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/xtensa/esp32: apply the APP CPU reset synchronously under
 icount

TBI Lab determinism fix (Sprint 1, carried to the maintained baseline in R1).
Releasing the APP CPU from reset went through qemu_system_reset_request(),
serviced asynchronously by the main loop, so the instruction at which CPU 1
restarted depended on host scheduling. Under -icount both vCPUs run on one
round-robin thread, so the same four steps are applied synchronously.
PRO CPU and full-system resets are unchanged (unsupported in V1).

Signed-off-by: TBI Lab <runtime@tbi-lab.local>
---
 hw/xtensa/esp32.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/xtensa/esp32.c b/hw/xtensa/esp32.c
index e000470..8e98eb6 100644
--- a/hw/xtensa/esp32.c
+++ b/hw/xtensa/esp32.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
  */
 
 #include "qemu/osdep.h"
+#include "sysemu/cpu-timers.h"
 #include "qemu/log.h"
 #include "qemu/error-report.h"
 #include "qemu/units.h"
@@ -109,6 +110,22 @@ static void esp32_dig_reset(void *opaque, int n, int level)
 static void esp32_cpu_reset(void* opaque, int n, int level)
 {
     Esp32SocState *s = ESP32_SOC(opaque);
+    if (level && n == 1 && icount_enabled()) {
+        /*
+         * TBI Lab determinism fix. An APP CPU reset only touches CPU 1 (see the
+         * ESP32_SOC_RESET_APPCPU branch of esp32_soc_reset). Routing it through
+         * qemu_system_reset_request() defers it to the main loop, so the instruction at
+         * which CPU 1 restarts -- and therefore the whole dual-core interleaving from
+         * then on -- depends on host scheduling. Under -icount every vCPU runs on one
+         * round-robin thread, so CPU 1 is not executing here and the same steps can be
+         * applied synchronously, at a point fixed by the guest's instruction stream.
+         */
+        s->rtc_cntl.reset_cause[1] = ESP32_SW_CPU_RESET;
+        xtensa_select_static_vectors(&s->cpu[1].env, s->rtc_cntl.stat_vector_sel[1]);
+        remove_cpu_watchpoints(&s->cpu[1]);
+        cpu_reset(CPU(&s->cpu[1]));
+        return;
+    }
     if (level) {
         s->requested_reset = (n == 0) ? ESP32_SOC_RESET_PROCPU : ESP32_SOC_RESET_APPCPU;
         /* Use different cause for APP CPU so that its reset doesn't cause QEMU to exit,
